WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.615
A hiring team needs course completion, not your whole certificate. In Facet, I upload training notes

00:00:06.615 --> 00:00:08.683
into a password-protected local vault.

00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:17.009
I create a private document NFT. The original stays on my device. This self-recorded fingerprint is

00:00:17.009 --> 00:00:20.411
explicitly unverified; uploading does not authenticate it.

00:00:20.961 --> 00:00:28.089
Now I issue a fictional training credential from Facet Test Academy. Its claims have a real BBS

00:00:28.089 --> 00:00:30.604
signature and a holder-bound NEAR record.

00:00:31.154 --> 00:00:37.679
The example hiring team requests just the course and completion status. The request binds its

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:41.159
purpose, selected fields and a short-lived, single-use nonce.

00:00:41.709 --> 00:00:47.675
Before approving, I can see exactly what is shared. Course and completion are revealed. The name,

00:00:47.675 --> 00:00:50.285
score, date and student reference stay hidden.

00:00:50.835 --> 00:00:57.505
I approve and export the proof. The real exported package excludes the hidden name and score, and

00:00:57.505 --> 00:00:59.859
includes the required public credential bindings.

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:06.624
In a separate browser, the verifier imports my proof without opening a vault or receiving the

00:01:06.624 --> 00:01:09.732
original document. Then they run the verification checks.

00:01:10.282 --> 00:01:17.292
The proof checks out. The verifier sees Data Privacy Foundations and Completed. A genuine NEAR

00:01:17.292 --> 00:01:20.095
transaction records the single-use verification receipt.

00:01:20.645 --> 00:01:27.627
The checks cover signatures, holder approval and live status. NEAR anchors issuer keys, expiry,

00:01:27.627 --> 00:01:32.613
revocation and nonce use. BBS math runs outside the contract.

00:01:33.163 --> 00:01:39.364
The proof lab runs real positive and negative checks, including altered claims and holder

00:01:39.364 --> 00:01:44.235
substitution. All eight behave as expected. Used requests cannot be replayed.

00:01:44.785 --> 00:01:51.374
I generate a fresh unused proof, then revoke the credential. Revocation changes its current NEAR

00:01:51.374 --> 00:01:54.449
status. It cannot erase information already shared.

00:01:54.999 --> 00:02:01.378
That unused proof now fails specifically because of revocation. Facet demonstrates useful credential

00:02:01.378 --> 00:02:08.247
NFTs with controlled disclosure. Try the live testnet app and inspect the public code.
